div.p:
A trade message worked out by Ray Price and my staff, which has been cleared with
                    all interested parties, is at Tab B. It explains the provisions of the bill and
                    sets forth the main elements of your trade policy: 

—that the U.S. remains committed to freer trade;

—but that new features of the world economy justify thorough
                            investigation by a Commission on World Trade before the U.S. decides on
                            any broad new trade policy for the 1970s;

—that our trade policy must be in our own interest—defined as our
                            larger interest and not a cover for special interests;

—that our reduced trade surplus heightens the need to move toward
                            freer trade;

—that textiles are a special problem, which should not deflect us from
                            our larger goals.
